# Conditions

`conditions` says which of the items the trigger fires on count. `match` is `all` (every condition) or `any` (any condition); `of` lists the conditions, and groups of them one level deep (a group holds no group), at most 50 in all. With none, write `{"match": "all", "of": []}`.

```json
{"match": "all", "of": [{"field": "pr.draft", "op": "isNo"}, {"group": {"match": "any", "of": [{"field": "item.labels", "op": "hasAny", "values": ["bug"]}, {"field": "pr.lines", "op": "lt", "value": "400"}]}}]}
```

A condition names a `field` (topic `fields`), an `op` its type takes, and what the op compares against: one value in `value`, several in `values`, or none. Each value is one line of at most 200 characters, at most 50 of them. A condition on a field that doesn't apply to an item, such as a pull request's field on an issue, is no; one on a value not read yet waits until it is read.

## `text`

One piece of text, compared ignoring case. A pattern takes `*` for any run of characters and `?` for one; a regex is Go's (RE2), ignoring case.

- `is`: one value, in `value`. Its opposite is `isNot`.
- `isNot` (isn't): one value, in `value`. Its opposite is `is`.
- `contains`: one value, in `value`. Its opposite is `notContains`.
- `notContains` (doesn't contain): one value, in `value`. Its opposite is `contains`.
- `startsWith` (starts with): one value, in `value`.
- `matches` (matches the pattern): one value, in `value`. * matches any run of characters and ? one, ignoring case.
- `matchesRegex` (matches the regex): one value, in `value`. A Go (RE2) regular expression, ignoring case.

## `list`

A list of text, such as the labels. A list whose values are a field's choices takes only `hasAny`, `hasAll`, `hasNone`, `isEmpty`, and `notEmpty`; one of paths matches its patterns as `.gitignore` does, the others as globs. An "all" operator over an empty list is no, never yes.

- `hasAny` (has any of): one or more, in `values`. Its opposite is `hasNone`.
- `hasAll` (has all of): one or more, in `values`. Holds every one of them; an empty list holds none, so it doesn't match.
- `hasNone` (has none of): one or more, in `values`. Its opposite is `hasAny`.
- `anyContains` (contains any of): one or more, in `values`. Its opposite is `noneContains`.
- `noneContains` (contains none of): one or more, in `values`. Its opposite is `anyContains`.
- `anyMatches` (any matches): one or more, in `values`.
- `allMatch` (all match): one or more, in `values`. Every one matches one of the patterns; an empty list doesn't match, so a pull request that changes no file never has all its paths under docs/.
- `isEmpty` (is empty): no value. Its opposite is `notEmpty`.
- `notEmpty` (isn't empty): no value. Its opposite is `isEmpty`.

## `number`

A whole number, written as its digits in a string: `"400"`.

- `eq` (=): one value, in `value`. Its opposite is `ne`.
- `ne` (≠): one value, in `value`. Its opposite is `eq`.
- `lt` (<): one value, in `value`. Its opposite is `gte`.
- `lte` (≤): one value, in `value`. Its opposite is `gt`.
- `gt` (>): one value, in `value`. Its opposite is `lte`.
- `gte` (≥): one value, in `value`. Its opposite is `lt`.

## `bool`

Yes or no; its operators take no value.

- `isYes` (is yes): no value. Its opposite is `isNo`.
- `isNo` (is no): no value. Its opposite is `isYes`.

## `choice`

One of the field's choices (topic `fields`), compared ignoring case.

- `oneOf` (is any of): one or more, in `values`. Its opposite is `noneOf`.
- `noneOf` (is none of): one or more, in `values`. Its opposite is `oneOf`.

## `duration`

How long ago something happened, against a length: a whole number of minutes, hours, or days, such as `"30m"`, `"2h"`, or `"14d"`.

- `moreThan` (more than): one value, in `value`.
- `lessThan` (less than): one value, in `value`.
